> For a singleton I would just expect 1 and then no further output (like with a manually created singleton transformer), but the "once per request" resolution change sounds like it would yield 1, 2, 3 etc.

Hmmm, the way I was seeing it, type-based transformers always have a scoped lifetime when they are registered ([similar to middleware](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/middleware/extensibility?view=aspnetcore-8.0), for example) since there is no way to explicitly associate a lifetime with a registered transformer.

The current bug is that schema/operation transformers violate this expectation because they end up being called once for each operation or schema respectively.

However, I would expect that singleton dependencies resolved within a transformer respect that lifetime.

```
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder();

builder.Services.AddSingleton<Dependency>();
builder.Services.AddOpenApi(options =>
{
	options.AddSchemaTransformer<SchemaTransformerWithDependency>();
});

// Instanitated for each request to the document service
public class SchemaTransformerWithDependency(Dependency dependency) : IOpenApiSchemaTransformer
{
	// ..
}

// Instantiated once for the lifetime of the application, regardless of how many times the
// Open API document is generated
public class Dependency { }
```

The transformer itself has a scoped, not singleton, lifetime. If we take a bit more inspiration from `IMiddlewareFactory`, it _does_ allow you to register middleware as either scoped or transient. So we could look into transformers with a transient scope but I dunno how valuable that would be.

Another gotcha here is the resolution of transient services in the middleware. Because we resolve the transformer once-per-request, transient services effectively have a scoped lifetime. Middlware works around this by allowing you to inject transient services into the `InvokeAsync` call so that you get a new instance every time the middleware is applied, or in our case every time `TransformAsync` is invoked.

So, there's a spectrum for how sophistcated we want to get with managing scopes in the factory-actived transformers here. We can go as far as replicating what we do with middleware or stick with the currrent convention which is:

- Transformers always have a scoped lifetime where the "unit-of-work" is a single request to generate the OpenAPI document.
- Transformers respect the singleton lifetime of dependencies they take with that lifetime.
- Transformers respect the scoped lifetime of dependencies they take with that lifetime.
- Transformers do not respect the transient lifetime of dependencies they take on (those are always effectively scoped).
